        "question": {
          ":question/id": "91511dc9-38f8-5065-99bc-ca49b0d57537",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
          ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=4x+1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (10,\\infty)$\n- **III.** $x\\in [-5,5)$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The function $f(x)$ is a linear function with slope $4$ and $y$-intercept $1,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/d1b3ba568b7a61246bcbf173a861fc04a2dff4de1a26a3b56a2f4c958283d006-0.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ On this interval, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n- Since the function is invertible on $x\\in (-\\infty,\\infty),$ it is also invertible on  $x\\in (10,\\infty)$ and $x\\in [-5,5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
