        "question": {
          ":question/id": "6ca093f8-800c-5949-b669-c293a37846e0",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x+\\frac{11}{4}|+2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in [- \\frac{7}{2},-3)$\n- **II.** $x\\in (- \\frac{13}{4},- \\frac{11}{4}]$\n- **III.** $x\\in (-3,- \\frac{5}{2}]$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=- \\frac{11}{4}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $[- \\frac{7}{2},-3)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $-3\\le - \\frac{11}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(- \\frac{13}{4},- \\frac{11}{4}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $- \\frac{11}{4}\\le - \\frac{11}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $(-3,- \\frac{5}{2}]$, contains the distinct inputs $- \\frac{23}{8}$ and $- \\frac{21}{8}$, both with output $\\frac{17}{8}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
