        "question": {
          ":question/id": "3670f115-c4d2-548f-bef8-c67687aae74b",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=(x-\\frac{9}{4})^{2}+3,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (2,\\frac{5}{2}]$\n- **II.** $x\\in (\\frac{7}{4},\\frac{9}{4}]$\n- **III.** $x\\in [\\frac{3}{2},2)$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it is one-to-one on that interval.\n\nThe vertex occurs at $x=\\frac{9}{4}$. On either side of this vertex, the function is strictly monotone. Including the vertex as an endpoint does not create a repeated output. An interval containing points on both sides is not one-to-one: inputs equally far from the vertex have the same output.\n\n- Interval I, $(2,\\frac{5}{2}]$, contains the distinct inputs $\\frac{17}{8}$ and $\\frac{19}{8}$, both with output $\\frac{193}{64}$. It is therefore not invertible.\n\n- Interval II, $(\\frac{7}{4},\\frac{9}{4}]$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $\\frac{9}{4}\\le \\frac{9}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\n- Interval III, $[\\frac{3}{2},2)$, lies on one side of the vertex, since $2\\le \\frac{9}{4}$. It is therefore invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\".",
