          "title": "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"
        },
        "question": {
          ":question/id": "6f215a5b-68b8-519d-a366-d4945889be76",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
          ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=|x-4|-1,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\in (-\\infty,4]$\n\n- **II.** $x\\in (4,\\infty)$\n\n- **III.** $x\\in (-\\infty,5]$",
          ":question/worked-solution": "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-4|-1$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n1\\. Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n2\\. Translate it by $4$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-4|.$\n\n3\\. Finally, translate $y=|x-4|$ by $1$ unit down to get $f(x)=|x-4|-1.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/17c74380b6bcea9b7cd5675ad48eae8affb9f10467d65e3fef15c6e39f3a207b-0.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty,4]$ and $x\\in (4,\\infty).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/17c74380b6bcea9b7cd5675ad48eae8affb9f10467d65e3fef15c6e39f3a207b-1.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty,5].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186124474/plots/17c74380b6bcea9b7cd5675ad48eae8affb9f10467d65e3fef15c6e39f3a207b-2.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and II only\".",
